This repository holds two different things under two different terms, and the
released app contains a third. Read all three paragraphs before you reuse
anything here.

The iOS integration -- the Swift sources, the XcodeGen spec, the build and test
scripts, the tests themselves -- is ours and is offered under the MIT licence
below.

The engine is not ours. It is openbst by Stanislaw Przedzinkowski, whose C code
is also MIT: https://github.com/Mudb0y/openbst. That licence is retained below;
openbst's own LICENSE governs its code.

The engine's **tables are neither ours nor his to license.** They are read out
of Berkeley Speech Technologies' binaries -- the letter-to-sound rules, the
dictionaries, the phoneme and duration tables, the excitation and gain tables of
the lattice -- so that the library can speak without those binaries present.
That data is Berkeley Speech Technologies' work, and later HumanWare's, which is
what openbst's LICENSE says in its first paragraph:

    That data is Berkeley Speech Technologies' work, and later HumanWare's.
    The MIT licence below covers our code and says nothing about their tables,
    which we are in no position to license to anyone. Use them where you have
    the right to.

The repository therefore carries no compiled tables: the XCFramework is
gitignored and built locally by `build_frameworks.py`. **The released `.ipa`,
however, does contain them**, because an installable app cannot do otherwise.
See NOTICE for what that means if you download it. Use the tables where you have
the right to.

